  "modeDesc.GREEK_TEXT": "Read the Greek text itself; tap a word to see its meaning in this passage",
  "page.howto": "\n        <div class=\"static-page\">\n\n            <section>\n                <h3>How to Use</h3>\n                <p>Bible Green is a Bible app that lets you read the New Testament in English, all the way to the nuance of the Greek original \\u2014 no Greek required. There are just three things to do.</p>\n            </section>\n\n            <section>\n                <h3>1. Tap a verse</h3>\n                <p>Tap a verse in the chapter you are reading, and its words line up in the original order.</p>\n            </section>\n\n            <section>\n                <h3>2. Tap a word</h3>\n                <p>Tap a word to see its meaning and what it is doing in the sentence. To go deeper, \\u201cLook up this word\\u2019s meaning and usage\\u201d opens the diagram of the sentence and the word\\u2019s dictionary meanings.</p>\n            </section>\n\n            <section>\n                <h3>3. Change how you read</h3>\n                <p>You can change the reading mode from the menu\\u2019s \\u201cReading Mode\\u201d.</p>\n                <div class=\"card\">\n                    <div class=\"aspect-row\"><span class=\"aspect-label\">Read in the World English Bible</span><span class=\"aspect-desc\">Start by reading straight through in a familiar English translation</span></div>\n                    <div class=\"aspect-row\"><span class=\"aspect-label\">Word Order</span><span class=\"aspect-desc\">Follow the original order and check the English, word by word</span></div>\n                    <div class=\"aspect-row\"><span class=\"aspect-label\">Sentence Structure</span><span class=\"aspect-desc\">Reorder the sentence along its structure, then read</span></div>\n                </div>\n            </section>\n\n            <section>\n                <h3>Reading the diagram</h3>\n                <p>The diagram in the word analysis shows how the word you picked relates to the other words in the sentence. \\u201cHow to read the marks\\u201d, at the top of the diagram, explains the symbols.</p>\n            </section>\n\n            <section>\n                <h3>Try it yourself</h3>\n                <p>A guided tour walks you through famous passages in Romans and John\\u2019s Gospel.</p>\n                <div class=\"back-wrap\">\n                    <button class=\"back-btn\" onclick=\"if (window.App && window.App.onboarding && window.App.onboarding.resetAndReload) window.App.onboarding.resetAndReload();\">Take the guided tour</button>\n                </div>\n            </section>\n\n            <div class=\"back-wrap\">\n                <button class=\"back-btn\" onclick=\"renderCurrentPage()\">Back to the text</button>\n            </div>\n\n        </div>\n    ",
  "page.appinfo": "\n        <div class=\"static-page\">\n\n            <section>\n                <h3>Design Philosophy</h3>\n                <p>This app aims to bring the Greek text of the New Testament closer to the experience of reading. Rather than leaning on grammatical terminology, it shows the aspect of verbs \\u2014 the quality of an action as it unfolds in time \\u2014 with visual marks, so that the nuance of the original can be felt intuitively.</p>\n                <div class=\"card\">\n                    <div class=\"aspect-row\">\n                        <span class=\"aspect-label\"><span class=\"present-text\">Solid line</span>\\u3000<span class=\"present-text\">Present</span></span>\n                        <span class=\"aspect-desc\">The action is still going on. It marks repetition, or something in progress.</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"aspect-row\">\n                        <span class=\"aspect-label\"><span class=\"aorist-text\">Dotted line</span>\\u3000<span class=\"aorist-text\">Aorist</span></span>\n                        <span class=\"aspect-desc\">Something that happened as one event. Not a continuing past, but a point of fact.</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"aspect-row\">\n                        <span class=\"aspect-label\"><span class=\"perfect-text\">Wavy line</span>\\u3000<span class=\"perfect-text\">Perfect</span></span>\n                        <span class=\"aspect-desc\">The result of something that happened still remains. Past and present stay connected.</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"aspect-row\">\n                        <span class=\"aspect-label\"><span class=\"future-text\">Double line</span>\\u3000<span class=\"future-text\">Future</span></span>\n                        <span class=\"aspect-desc\">Something that will certainly happen \\u2014 or the certainty and will to bring it about.</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"aspect-row\">\n                        <span class=\"aspect-label\">*\\u3000Middle / Passive</span>\n                        <span class=\"aspect-desc\">Shows whether the action extends to the subject itself (middle), or is received from outside (passive).</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"aspect-row\">\n                        <span class=\"aspect-label\"><span class=\"indicative-text\">Background color</span>\\u3000<span class=\"indicative-text\">Indicative / Imperative</span></span>\n                        <span class=\"aspect-desc\">The verbs that carry the sentence: statements of fact, or strong calls to action.</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </section>\n\n            <section>\n                <h3>About the Japanese in This App (Reading Japanese)</h3>\n                <p>The Japanese shown in this app is not an ordinary \\u201ctranslation\\u201d. It is Japanese for reading the structure of the Greek text \\u2014 number, gender, person, case, deixis, and more.</p>\n                <p>It reflects only what can be determined from the text itself, such as form and structure, and never fills gaps by guesswork or settles a word\\u2019s meaning one-sidedly. The original text stays the starting point, in a form you can follow by reading.</p>\n                <p>The full thinking is published as the \\u201cReading Japanese Specification\\u201d.</p>\n            </section>\n\n            <section>\n                <h3>Sources</h3>\n                <table>\n                    <tr><th>Greek Text</th><td>SBL Greek New Testament (SBLGNT)<br><small>\\u00a9 Faithlife / Logos Bible Software, edited by Michael W. Holmes \\u2014 CC BY 4.0</small></td></tr>\n                    <tr><th>Morphological Analysis</th><td>MACULA Greek Linguistic Datasets, available at <a href=\"https://github.com/Clear-Bible/macula-greek/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://github.com/Clear-Bible/macula-greek/</a><br><small>\\u00a9 Biblica, Inc. \\u2014 CC BY 4.0</small></td></tr>\n                    <tr><th>English Glosses</th><td>Cherith Glosses (Andi Wu)<br><small>\\u00a9 2023 Cherith Analytics \\u2014 CC BY 4.0</small></td></tr>\\n                    <tr><th>Lexicon</th><td>Abbott-Smith, <em>A Manual Greek Lexicon of the New Testament</em></td></tr>\n                    <tr>\n                      <th>Syntactic Classification</th>\n                      <td>\n                        Wallace, Daniel B. <em>Greek Grammar Beyond the Basics.</em> Zondervan, 1996.<br>\n                        <small>Wallace\\u2019s grammatical classification and methods of analysis serve as a reference; the explanations themselves are written independently, for Japanese readers.</small>\n                      </td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr>\n                      <th>Japanese Translations</th>\n                      <td>The Kougo (Colloquial) Version (Japan Bible Society, 1955) and the Bungo (Literary) Version (Japan Bible Society, revised 1917)</td>\n                    </tr>\n                </table>\n            </section>\n\n            <section>\n                <h3>Privacy</h3>\n                <p>This app does not currently send or collect personal information or access logs on any server. Your reading progress and onboarding state are stored only in your browser\\u2019s localStorage, and are never sent to a server.</p>\n                <p>If a future feature ever requires collecting information, that change will be announced in advance and made only with your consent.</p>\n            </section>\n\n            <section>\n                <h3>On the Use of AI</h3>\n                <p>No AI is used in any of the core reading functions of this app: displaying the biblical text, morphological analysis, syntactic information, or dictionary lookup.</p>\n                <p>The Greek text, Japanese text, word-form information, grammatical information, and dictionary and search data shown in the app are all based on scholarly data sources.</p>\n                <p>AI is used only as coding support during development, and as an aid in translating and preparing dictionary data.</p>\n                <p>The biblical text and grammatical information provided in this app are therefore not generated by AI.</p>\n            </section>\n\n            <section>\n                <h3>About This Project</h3>\n                <p>Bible Green is a non-commercial project, run by an individual. \\u00a9 2026 Hiroki Minamida</p>\n            </section>\n\n            <div class=\"back-wrap\">\n                <button class=\"back-btn\" onclick=\"renderCurrentPage()\">Back to the text</button>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    ",
